<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;margin-bottom:16px"><span style="padding:2px 10px;border-radius:4px;background:rgba(244,114,182,.15);color:#f9a8d4;font-size:.7em;font-weight:700">🔬 Scientist</span></div>
<h2>Algebraic Geometry — Varieties, Schemes, and the Geometry of Polynomials</h2>
<p style="font-size:.95em;color:var(--text-sec);margin-bottom:16px">Algebraic geometry studies solutions of polynomial equations. It spans affine and projective varieties, schemes (Grothendieck), sheaves, cohomology (Čech, sheaf, étale), intersection theory, and moduli spaces. It's one of the most active and abstract fields of modern mathematics, with deep connections to number theory, topology, and physics.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"Algebraic geometry seems to have acquired the reputation of being esoteric, exclusive, and very abstract, but it is actually quite concrete and relevant to many fields."</div><div class="attribution">— David Mumford</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>🌾 Affine and Projective Varieties</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Zero Sets</div><div class="story-title">From Polynomial Equations to Geometric Objects</div><div class="story-text">An <strong>affine variety</strong> V(I) ⊂ 𝔸ⁿ is the set of common zeros of an ideal I ⊂ k[x₁,...,xₙ]. The <strong>Zariski topology</strong> has closed sets = algebraic sets. <strong>Hilbert's Nullstellensatz</strong>: I(V(J)) = √J — the radical of J. This is the fundamental dictionary between ideals and varieties. A variety is <strong>irreducible</strong> if it's not a union of two proper closed subsets — equivalently, its coordinate ring k[V] = k[x]/I(V) is an integral domain. The <strong>dimension</strong> is the transcendence degree of the function field k(V). <strong>Projective varieties</strong> live in ℙⁿ — defined by homogeneous polynomials. They're proper (complete) — the algebraic analogue of compactness. <strong>Morphisms</strong> are polynomial maps between varieties. The <strong>blow-up</strong> resolves singularities by replacing a point with a ℙ^{n−1}. Hironaka's theorem: every variety over ℂ admits a resolution of singularities.</div><div class="story-moral">The Nullstellensatz is the Rosetta Stone — it translates algebra (ideals) into geometry (varieties) and back.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🏗️ Schemes — Grothendieck's Revolution</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Schemes</div><div class="story-title">Affine Schemes, Structure Sheaves, and Fiber Products</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>scheme</strong> is a locally ringed space locally isomorphic to Spec R (set of prime ideals of R). This unifies varieties (R = finitely generated k-algebra) and arithmetic geometry (R = ℤ, ℚ). The <strong>structure sheaf</strong> O_X assigns to each open set the ring of regular functions. A <strong>morphism</strong> of schemes is a continuous map + a sheaf map. The <strong>fiber product</strong> X ×_S Y is the categorical product over S — it's the basis for base change. <strong>Separated</strong> and <strong>proper</strong> morphisms are the algebraic analogs of Hausdorff and compact. A scheme is <strong>Noetherian</strong> if covered by finitely many Spec of Noetherian rings. <strong>Flat morphisms</strong> are the algebraic version of continuously varying families. The <strong>Hilbert scheme</strong> parameterizes subschemes of a given scheme with fixed Hilbert polynomial — it's the moduli space of subschemes.</div><div class="story-moral">Schemes allow polynomial equations to be studied over any ring — including ℤ — making algebraic geometry the natural language of arithmetic geometry.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🧮 Sheaf Cohomology and Intersection Theory</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Cohomology</div><div class="story-title">Čech Cohomology, Serre Duality, and the Riemann-Roch Theorem</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Sheaf cohomology</strong> Hⁱ(X,ℱ) measures the obstructions to gluing local sections. <strong>Čech cohomology</strong> computes it using open covers. The <strong>Euler characteristic</strong> χ(ℱ) = Σ (−1)ⁱ dim Hⁱ is finite for coherent sheaves on proper schemes. <strong>Serre duality</strong>: Hⁱ(X, ℱ) ≅ H^{n−i}(X, ℱ^* ⊗ ω_X)^* for smooth projective varieties, where ω_X is the canonical sheaf. The <strong>Riemann-Roch theorem</strong> for curves: dim H⁰(𝒪(D)) − dim H⁰(𝒪(K−D)) = deg D + 1 − g. The <strong>Hirzebruch-Riemann-Roch theorem</strong> generalizes to higher dimensions using Chern classes: χ(ℱ) = ∫_X ch(ℱ) td(T_X). The <strong>Grothendieck-Riemann-Roch</strong> applies to proper morphisms. <strong>Intersection theory</strong> (Fulton-MacPherson) defines intersection products using Chow groups A_*(X) and the moving lemma. The <strong>Mumford-Castelnuovo regularity</strong> bounds the complexity of sheaves.</div><div class="story-moral">Sheaf cohomology and the Riemann-Roch theorem count the number of functions with prescribed zeros and poles — the basic numerical tool of algebraic geometry.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🔬 Moduli Spaces and Modern Developments</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Parameters</div><div class="story-title">Moduli Spaces, GIT Quotients, and the Minimal Model Program</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Moduli spaces</strong> parameterize geometric objects — curves, vector bundles, sheaves. The moduli space of curves M_g has dimension 3g−3. The <strong>Mumford-MacDonald formula</strong> computes its cohomology. <strong>Geometric Invariant Theory (GIT)</strong> constructs quotients of group actions — stable points correspond to good orbits. The <strong>Minimal Model Program</strong> (Birkar-Cascini-Hacon-McKernan) classifies algebraic varieties up to birational equivalence — every variety is birational to a minimal model (K_X nef) or a Mori fiber space. <strong>Calabi-Yau manifolds</strong> have trivial canonical bundle (ω_X = 𝒪_X) and are central to mirror symmetry. <strong>Stable curves</strong> (nodal curves with finite automorphism group) compactify M_g. The <strong>moduli of vector bundles</strong> uses slope stability (Mumford-Takemoto) and Gieseker stability. The <strong>Langlands program</strong> in geometric form connects moduli of bundles on curves to representations of the fundamental group.</div><div class="story-moral">Moduli spaces turn classification into geometry — the set of all curves or bundles becomes a space with its own rich structure.</div></div>
